      },
      "text": "**Anthropometry** = measurement of the human body for design purposes. The classic problem:"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md#008",
      "path": [
        "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating, git-history:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating]",
        "canonical_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "current_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "id": "maxim:furniture:ergonomics-seating",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "furniture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "section": "furniture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ergonomics of Seating",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nPERCENTILE RANGES (approximate, mixed gender):\n  Dimension           5th percentile    50th percentile    95th percentile\n  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Seated height        775mm             870mm              970mm\n  Eye height seated    660mm             770mm              865mm\n  Shoulder width       355mm             430mm              490mm\n  Seated elbow height  185mm             240mm              290mm\n  Popliteal height     365mm             430mm              490mm\n    (floor to behind knee)\n  Seat-to-shoulder     490mm             570mm              640mm\n  Buttock-popliteal    430mm             480mm              530mm\n    depth (seat depth needed)"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md#009",
      "path": [
        "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating, git-history:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating]",
        "canonical_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "current_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "id": "maxim:furniture:ergonomics-seating",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "furniture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "section": "furniture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ergonomics of Seating",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "\"AVERAGE\" IS WRONG:\n  If you design a chair for the \"average\" person (50th percentile):\n  50% of users will have legs too long or too short.\n  50% will have backs too tall or too short.\n  The \"average\" person matches NO single individual — it's a statistical\n  centroid that doesn't exist in reality."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md#010",
      "path": [
        "08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating, git-history:furniture:08-ergonomics-seating]",
        "canonical_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "current_path": "furniture/08-ERGONOMICS-SEATING.md",
        "id": "maxim:furniture:ergonomics-seating",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "furniture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ergonomics, seating]",
        "section": "furniture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ergonomics of Seating",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The design response: adjustability, or designing for extremes\n  with clear target use case (airline economy = maximize capacity,\n  accept discomfort; executive chair = maximize adjustability).\n```"
    }
